      "answer": "A CUDA warp is a group of 32 threads that the GPU schedules together. Threads run the same program on different data; when they take different branches, the GPU executes the different paths with only the relevant lanes active, which can reduce efficiency. Warps are formed within a thread block, and you should use explicit synchronization when threads need to coordinate rather than assuming they always execute in lockstep.",
